Let $f$ and $g$ be defined on $[a,b]$ and differentiable at a point $x\in[a,b]$. Then $f+g$ and $fg$ are differentiable at $x$, and so is $f/g$ provided $g(x)\neq0$; moreover
(a) $(f+g)'(x)=f'(x)+g'(x)$;
(b) $(fg)'(x)=f'(x)\,g(x)+f(x)\,g'(x)$;
(c) $\left(\dfrac fg\right)'(x)=\dfrac{g(x)\,f'(x)-g'(x)\,f(x)}{g(x)^2}$.

Suppose $X,Y\ge0$, or $E|X|<\infty$ and $E|Y|<\infty$. Then

(a) $E(X+Y)=EX+EY$;
(b) $E(aX+b)=a\,EX+b$ for all real $a,b$;
(c) if $X\ge Y$, then $EX\ge EY$.
